@@ -61,6 +61,24 @@ Usage
     >>> requirements1 == requirements2
     True
 
+Use :meth:`Requirement.__replace__` to copy a requirement with selected fields
+changed. It accepts keyword arguments ``name``, ``url``, ``extras``,
+``specifier``, and ``marker``, with the same types as the attributes:
+
+.. doctest::
+
+    >>> req = Requirement('requests[security]>=2.0; python_version >= "3"')
+    >>> req.__replace__(name="flask")
+    <Requirement('flask[security]>=2.0; python_version >= "3"')>
+
+On Python 3.13 and later, this also supports :func:`copy.replace`.
+Omitted fields are retained; calling without arguments returns an equal,
+distinct object. Invalid requirements and field values that would be
+reinterpreted by parsing raise :class:`InvalidRequirement`. Unknown keywords
+raise :class:`TypeError`. Specifier prerelease overrides are preserved, and
+the copied specifier set and its members are independent of the originals.
+Subclasses are constructed through their own initializer.
+
 .. versionchanged:: 23.2
 
     When a requirement is specified with a URL, the :class:`Requirement` class
